Figure 2: Architecture of the proposed multi-modal transmission forecasting model.
The spatial block fuses four modalities:
(a) a camera encoder of 24 horizon-locked sky maps by residual ChebConv;
(b) a satellite encoder of native GOES ABI patches by dilated 2-D convolution, run once per unique $10$ min ABI scan and gathered onto the 24 camera times;
(c) a wind and metadata encoder, with multi-head attention over DMW point clouds and an MLP on hour and scan age;
(d) geometry fusion (pierce join) that samples the rectangular satellite features onto spherical sightlines at cloud-height decks.
The temporal block (e) mixes the 24-frame history with a forecast query by a masked dilated 1-D convolution and a lead-conditioned attention pool. History is encoded once. A shared decoder then emits a residual on last-frame optical depth at each requested lead (90 s, 5 min, 20 min, 30 min), using that lead’s embedding and wind-displaced GOES pierce. The output updates a persistence baseline via the Beer–Lambert law (Eq. 6).
